event('232-6.3-gluing','targeted proof check','Locally the union ring is the fibre product A/J1 times over A/(J1+J2) A/J2. Given two invertible modules and a matching isomorphism on the quotient, their fibre product is invertible: local bases may be adjusted by a lifted unit so that the identification is the identity, leaving the original fibre-product ring. This construction glues and is inverse to restriction. For fixed line bundles on the two pieces, the isomorphisms on proper flat Z form an affine scheme: represent the two Hom functors by affine linear schemes and impose that the two compositions equal identity. Compatibility with the chosen rigidifications is a further closed equation. Proper-flat Hom representability and affine descent are the background inputs; the local gluing calculation and the inverse-pair description are checked here.','232, p. 159, opening paragraph completing 6.3')
event('232-6.4','targeted proof check','Induct on unions of the reduced components, applying 6.3 at each nonempty intersection, which is automatically flat over a field. To meet the H-zero condition, pass faithfully flatly to an algebraic closure and split each original component into its connected open-and-closed pieces. Their Picard schemes are factors of the assumed base-changed Picard schemes. Connected proper reduced unions have only scalar global functions; disconnected unions are products. The affine maps therefore compose, and affineness descends. Effectiveness of the representing-object descent is the background descent input, not a newly proved theorem here.','232, p. 159, corollaire 6.4')
event('232-6.5','targeted proof check','The nilpotent ideal has a finite filtration, reducing the argument to a square-zero thickening with ideal I. The exact sequence 1+I to O_X^* to O_X0^* identifies 1+I with the additive sheaf I. Its cohomology yields the H1(I) lifting space and H2(I) obstruction. These cohomology groups are finite-dimensional over k; their base-change functors are affine vector groups. Global units come from finite k-algebras and form affine algebraic groups, so the kernel is the quotient of the additive lifting group by the image of global units. The theorem that a quotient by a normal closed subgroup of a commutative affine algebraic group is affine is an explicit background input. The lift locus is the zero locus of the obstruction and the lifts are torsors under that affine kernel; affine descent then gives the claimed affine morphism. This checks the reduction mechanism, not a reconstruction of the cited Oort paper.','232, p. 159, proposition 6.5')
event('232-6.6-corollary','used and rechecked in this run','After algebraic closure, apply 6.5 to remove nilpotents, 6.4 to the reduced union of components, and 3.1 (or 6.2) to each integral projective component. This explains the stated projective existence consequence; field-descent effectiveness is used as background. The separate following remark is also numbered 6.6 in the print, so it is distinguished by its statement id rather than silently renumbered.','232, p. 159, corollaire 6.6')
event('232-6.6-remark','carried as background without fault found','The original text proposes general proper existence as plausible and starts a reduction using Chow lemma. Preserve that historical status and the repeated printed number 6.6. The later erratum announcement has been removed from the expose and will be checked in its own section. The argument continues on p. 160.','232, p. 159, remarque 6.6 opening')
